WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:06.109
Loads of fans, myself included, have been
absolutely loving Mamma Mia: Here We Go Again,

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:11.090
though something that has disappointed some
is Meryl Streep's miniscule role in the movie.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.810
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'm going to reveal the

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:20.099
real reason why we see so little Streep in
the sequel and I'll also explain the behind-the-scenes

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:22.610
decisions that led to that scene with Donna.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:28.060
Be aware there are spoilers ahead for Donna's
story in Mamma Mia 2 if you haven't seen it

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.060
yet.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.130
When the first trailer for Mamma Mia: Here
We Go Again dropped, speculation ran wild

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.520
that Donna was no longer alive.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.940
While Meryl Streep's appearance was teased
in some bits of new footage, the overall tone

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.290
suggested Donna had passed away.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:46.480
Indeed, Sophie addresses her mother's absence
right at the start of the new movie, and it's

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.170
revealed soon after that Donna has died.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.920
I'm glad the filmmakers ripped off that Band-Aid
instead of making us wait to find out Donna's

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.590
fate, though it did make for a somewhat melancholy
tone in some of the present-day scenes, and

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.190
it's a little frustrating we got no explanation
as to what happened.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:06.500
We do, of course, get a brilliant prequel
story with Lily James as the young Donna,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.259
but Meryl Streep herself doesn't appear in
person until the very final scene in the chapel

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:16.270
where she's a ghostly apparition only seen
by her daughter and then she also performs

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.390
in the Super Trouper song.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.010
Watching Streep and Amanda Seyfried sing 'My
Love, My Life' in that closing scene is a

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.500
very touching moment, as mother and daughter
are reunited one final time.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.270
However, it's also heart-breaking and has
had many fans crying their eyes out at the

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.270
cinema.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.950
So, why exactly did the filmmakers really
make this particular story choice?

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.030
Something that was kept pretty hush-hush during
the publicity tour for Mamma Mia 2 is that

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.939
Meryl Streep wasn't keen on doing a sequel.

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:47.979
Here We Go Again writer-director Ol Parker
has admitted that Streep's small role in the

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:50.320
new movie wasn't due to scheduling conflicts.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.520
In fact, he said that if Streep had taken
the main lead role in the sequel, then the

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.159
production would have moved its schedule to
accommodate her.

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:01.850
After all, they waited 10 years to do Mamma
Mia 2, so I doubt another 6 or 12 months would

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:02.850
have mattered.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.649
The main issue was that Streep didn't want
such a major part in any sequel.

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.000
Indeed, Mamma Mia creator Judy Craymer revealed
that Streep told her she would never take

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.090
on all that singing and running around on
clifftops again.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:19.200
And that's not because she didn't enjoy making
the first movie, it's just that during her

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.260
incredible career, the three-time Oscar-winning
actress has, until now, never made a sequel.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.040
Streep is one of Hollywood's most versatile
and successful actresses and she's rightly

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.260
willing to turn down roles if they don't peak
her interest enough.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.530
I certainly can't fault her for wanting to
keep her career and life fresh for herself,

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.989
and let's remember, of course, that on top
of that, she's 69 now, 10 years older than

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.180
when she made the original Mamma Mia.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.390
Still, Streep DID want "to be involved" in
the sequel in some way, so the challenge was

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:51.560
for the filmmakers to figure out a follow-up
story that worked with drastically less Donna.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.810
Ol Parker who was tasked with writing the
script for Mamma Mia 2 struggled initially,

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.680
coming up empty on ideas.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.950
Obviously, Donna isn't the only main character
in Mamma Mia, however, the core of the story

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:07.170
has always centred around her and her daughter
Sophie, so cutting out Donna left a big hole.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:11.160
The solution eventually came from Richard
Curtis's daughter who suggested they take

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.990
a cue from one of the most famous and best-ever
movie sequels, The Godfather Part 2, in other

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.439
words, do a prequel-meets-sequel.

00:03:19.439 --> 00:03:23.810
By writing a prequel story with a new young
cast, it meant that the much-loved character

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.719
of Donna could remain centre-stage in Mamma
Mia 2 without Streep actually appearing on

00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:30.660
screen for most of the movie.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.650
The original film had hinted at a rich backstory
for Donna which gave the filmmakers plenty

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.239
of ideas to explore in the Young Donna storyline.

00:03:38.239 --> 00:03:42.830
As for how Streep's small part in the movie
would work, Ol Parker decided that the "most

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.799
meaningful and emotional and impactful way"
would be for Donna to die.

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:50.799
That decision was discussed with everyone
and Streep was reported to be "thrilled and

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:53.670
delighted" with passing the baton to the younger
cast.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.510
Now, your feelings on whether the producers
made a good decision in killing off Donna

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.599
will vary, I'm sure.

00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:03.439
Ol Parker himself has said that he knew the
decision would be a radical and controversial

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:08.140
one and that the audience would decide whether
it was "radical good or radical bad."

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:12.780
Donna's passing is obviously felt throughout
the movie, from Rosie bursting into tears

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.939
when her name is mentioned to Sam singing
a plaintive 'S.O.S.'

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:18.370
by himself in contrast to the 'S.O.S.'

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.209
duet he sang with Meryl Streep in the first
movie.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:25.710
Amanda Seyfried said she thinks the sequel
is better, and called the first movie "a beach"

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.500
in comparison to Here We Go Again which she
described as "an ocean" in terms of its emotional

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.500
depth.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.380
And, I suspect, that the slightly darker tone
and story of Mamma Mia 2 might have played

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.080
better for critics in their reviews of this
film compared to the first one.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.360
As for me, I really enjoyed the new movie
though I have some mixed feelings.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.350
One part of me wishes they could have figured
out a clever story that kept Donna alive and

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.930
managed to have Meryl Streep appear for at
least a few more scenes.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.139
However, I realise that, both story-wise and
given the amount of time they had with Streep,

00:04:55.139 --> 00:04:59.380
that would have been a rather difficult feat
to pull off, without having some overly-convoluted

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.110
reasoning for why Sophie's mother was absent.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.640
Something that occurred to me is that in Mamma
Mia 2, young Donna's friends try to convince

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.880
her to go out and see the world rather than
stay on the island, so maybe that idea could

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.970
have worked for present-day Donna.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.920
Despite some melancholy moments then, the
sequel is still full of charm and has lots

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.720
of joyous, celebratory scenes, culminating
in the curtain-call performance of Super Trouper

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.819
where Meryl Streep joins all the old and new
cast for a final rousing number.

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:28.889
